  "boundaries": [],
  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Independent publication — local scope",
      "items": [
        "Review offers trusted objects without completed-product proof. Accepted/release targets retain proof and closeout. Zero, one or many independent remotes receive the same local OIDs; fresh peer checks preserve partial and unknown outcomes, never global atomicity."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Admission and result are different claims",
      "items": [
        "All required inputs: Authorized subject; Applicable authority; Git version substrate; Remote ref observation; Declared publication remote.",
        "Required claim selector: Exact trusted source object, introduced range, declared target role and peer-local refs; review needs no accepted-product proof, while accepted/release requires applicable proof and closeout. Re-derive obligations before each effect.",
        "Attestation is the sole durable semantic result, scoped by predicate, bindings, validity and verifier, never permission. Commitment, Facts and Plan are transient. Change relations and experiments are derived: no persistent workflow graph, compatibility residue, feedback ledger or memory-backed truth."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Destination roles determine obligations",
      "items": [
        "Review requires the exact trusted source object and introduced commit range; it does not require a candidate checkout, completed Change proof or premature archive. Accepted/release targets retain applicable proof, intent and closeout. A mixed publication satisfies every selected destination.",
        "Exact prior policy classifies an unaccepted destination. Proposed policy cannot turn a protected target into review. Detached CI observes the same Git inputs without a host Lease, local mutation or authority issuance.",
        "Before each effect or already-matching claim, recheck source trust, applicable proof, target policy and exact peer-local old refs. An earlier peer success or equality observation does not authorize reuse. The request digest binds intent, not reusable permission.",
        "Keep confirmed effects if another peer blocks. Missing observations remain UNKNOWN; resume through fresh admission. Each peer applies its selected full-ref set atomically; the collection of peers is not one transaction."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Refusal and uncertainty are different",
      "items": [
        "A stale effect-time check admits no runner. A definite CAS mismatch means this local CAS did not update its ref; it does not undo earlier effects or other resources. An uncertain outcome must be observed before any further attempt.",
        "Contradiction or missing meaning blocks the affected effect. Preserve the counterexample, promote the smallest model boundary and recompile; no exception route around authority."
      ]
    }
  ]
